WEBVTT

00:00.000 --> 00:09.000
This is the advanced features demonstration for Group nil for the embedded coursework 2.

00:09.900 --> 00:14.800
On the user interface, at the moment the vibrato is set to 5, so let me just put that back to 0.

00:15.900 --> 00:19.900
As you can see, this knob will do the volume and you can see the change on the user interface

00:19.950 --> 00:26.650
as you increase or decrease: increase by going clockwise, decrease by going anti-clockwise.

00:26.650 --> 00:28.650
And that is the case for all of the knobs.

00:28.650 --> 00:30.000
That is the way that they are incremented or decremented.

00:30.000 --> 00:37.100
So this is a sawtooth wave currently, as is displayed on the screen, and this is what it sounds like.

00:37.100 --> 00:39.900
[Sawtooth notes playing]

00:39.900 --> 00:43.900
And you can see that when you press a button, the note is displayed on the screen,

00:43.900 --> 00:45.900
[Notes playing]

00:45.900 --> 00:49.900
like so. We've implemented polyphony, so when you press multiple notes,

00:49.900 --> 00:57.100
you hear a combined sound, and it works with any number of the notes on the keyboard.

00:57.100 --> 00:59.800
For example, I could do the whole thing.

00:59.900 --> 01:04.000
You can hear all of the notes.

01:04.000 --> 01:09.800
And then we have implemented a tremolo, so it is the first knob. If I turn it a little bit,

01:09.900 --> 01:19.800
you'll hear tremolo. If I turn it more, it will increase the frequency of the amplitude

01:19.900 --> 01:29.800
modulation, and so on as you increase, or less as you decrease. Then we also have the vibrato.

01:29.900 --> 01:39.800
So vibrato, it is a frequency modulation, as can be heard, and when you increase the vibrato

01:39.850 --> 01:44.000
number, you are increasing the frequency range but also the frequency of the

01:44.000 --> 01:48.000
modulation. So like this.

01:49.800 --> 01:59.800
Even higher. That's actually going faster, and when you go lower there's a

01:59.850 --> 02:07.400
much slower change. And these all work with polyphony, so to demonstrate.

02:09.800 --> 02:18.800
They also work in combination, and in combination with polyphony.

02:19.800 --> 02:29.700
Like so. And tremolo also works with polyphony, like so. We also have, obviously, the octave too,

02:29.750 --> 02:35.500
displayed on the screen. And now we can change the waveform. So if I take one rotation to

02:35.500 --> 02:39.700
the right, the waveform changes to sine, as indicated on the screen. And this is

02:39.750 --> 02:49.700
sine. And polyphony works with sine.

02:49.750 --> 02:59.700
Sine with any number of keys.

02:59.750 --> 03:09.700
And tremolo works with sine, in the same way as before: as you increase, it increases, and

03:09.700 --> 03:14.800
vibrato also works with sine.

03:14.800 --> 03:18.500
It works with sine and polyphony.

03:18.500 --> 03:19.700
It works with sine, polyphony

03:19.700 --> 03:29.300
and tremolo. So, as you can see, basically all of the features, they all work in

03:29.300 --> 03:31.600
conjunction.

03:31.600 --> 03:37.300
I will not be able to demonstrate them all right now, but they all work in conjunction.

03:37.300 --> 03:39.700
So then we have the triangle wave, which is the final

03:39.700 --> 03:49.600
waveform. It works normally and it also works with polyphony. Let me just increase the volume, sorry.

03:49.650 --> 03:55.100
And you can hear that it works.

03:55.100 --> 03:59.600
It also works with the tremolo.

03:59.650 --> 04:07.400
You can hear the tremolo, and obviously as you increase the tremolo, more, you know, increases

04:07.400 --> 04:09.600
the frequency of the

04:09.600 --> 04:19.500
amplitude modulation, and it also works with vibrato, and as you increase

04:19.600 --> 04:27.900
again, same effect as before, and as you can see, that also works with polyphony, and

04:27.900 --> 04:29.600
polyphony works with both.

04:29.600 --> 04:36.600
And it works with all three at the same time whilst being a triangle wave, and you can also

04:36.600 --> 04:39.400
change the volume, as we've heard, so

04:39.600 --> 04:49.100
or even lower, maybe five. Slightly quieter. And to demonstrate both at the same time.

04:49.550 --> 04:53.950
So triangle wave with vibrato and with tremolo and polyphony.

04:56.750 --> 04:59.400
They all work in conjunction. So

04:59.550 --> 05:06.200
to summarise, we have this knob which varies the tremolo, and this works no matter

05:06.200 --> 05:08.500
what other settings you have on the board.

05:09.500 --> 05:17.100
Whatever the settings you have defined, this tremolo will provide the tremolo effect, no

05:17.100 --> 05:18.700
matter what else it's being used with.

05:19.500 --> 05:24.800
We have vibrato, and that will work again no matter what other effects you are currently

05:24.800 --> 05:29.500
using, and whatever waveform you are currently using, it will add vibrato

05:29.500 --> 05:36.400
to that, and we can increase both of those as you turn more to the right, and

05:36.400 --> 05:39.500
decrease as you go towards the left, so anti-clockwise.

05:39.500 --> 05:46.000
Then we have this knob which varies the waveform, as I have shown, and it goes

05:46.000 --> 05:49.450
from sawtooth to sine wave to triangle

05:49.450 --> 05:56.100
wave. So the three waveforms. And the final knob will vary the volume, as I have shown.

05:56.100 --> 05:59.400
And all of these changes, as you can see,

05:59.450 --> 06:06.000
update the display, and you can visually see the changes, and as I have demonstrated

06:06.000 --> 06:09.400
you can also audibly hear the changes

06:09.400 --> 06:12.400
as I press the notes and add the effects.

06:13.400 --> 06:19.400
So these are the main advanced features in the demonstration.

06:19.400 --> 06:23.000
And thank you for watching.
